  ('2eb580d6-c48c-58fb-a238-dcbfa830f50b', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$In paragraph 5, the author writes, "it is better to be safe than sorry." What does this adage mean in this passage?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Stay outside until the rain actually starts to fall.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"},{"text":"Go inside early, even if the storm seems far away.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Lightning only strikes people who are being unsafe.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Say sorry to others if you get caught in the rain.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Go inside early, even if the storm seems far away.$q$, $q$The paragraph explains that "lightning can strike miles from the rain," so it is wiser to take shelter before trouble comes. Waiting for the rain is exactly the risk the adage warns against.$q$, 'draft', '03e0eb7d-ec8f-5b7a-bb86-45fc728e4fcf')
